  "solution": {
    "text": "<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/ijsbqqrqglbekvpvuxiodmlzkgthhxwxsllljbtqrykjzuujer.png\" /><br /><br />If we consider break down in Zener diode, then potential across <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math> will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> .<br /><br />So current in <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math> will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>1500</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>300</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math><br /><br />and current in <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>500</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math><br /><br /><math><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math> , which is not possible<br /><br /><math><mo>&#8658;</mo></math>&nbsp;The potential difference across the Zener diode does not reach to break down voltage. So no current will flow through the reverse-biased Zener diode.",
